# Line endings. `text` normalizes to LF at check-in; `eol` fixes what a
# checkout holds on every machine, also where core.autocrlf=true, so the
# working tree matches the index and a release built from it holds the same
# bytes as GitHub's archive of its commit (which v1.4.18's updater downloads;
# build_release.py checks this). Batch files are the deliberate exception:
# cmd.exe needs CRLF.
*.py    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# Release files that no extension rule above covers. Committed as LF.
/LICENSE                                        text eol=lf
/START_HERE.txt                                 text eol=lf
/hermes_gui.vbs                                 text eol=lf
portable_hermes/manifests/legacy-core-files.txt text eol=lf
portable_hermes/manifests/python-runtime.txt    text eol=lf

# tools/ and skills/ are left out of `git archive`, and so of GitHub's source
# ZIPs. A v1.4.18 install updates by downloading the repository's main-branch
# ZIP and replacing every top-level entry that ZIP contains. Without these
# two folders in it, the old core's tools/ (with tools\registry.py and any
# hand-placed tools\custom files) and skills/ stay whole, and Hermes keeps
# working until the next UPDATE.bat installs a release. Releases are built by
# build_release.py, which still ships the portable tools and skills.
# Because of this, a "Source code" download lacks the portable tools, and
# install.bat and START.bat say so instead of installing (exit code 12).
# These two lines can be removed once v1.4.18 installs no longer matter:
# source ZIPs are then complete again, and that message becomes unreachable.
/tools  export-ignore
/skills export-ignore
